{"title":"Infinitude of integers","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Infinitude of the Integers</b> is a fundamental theorem in number theory and mathematical logic stating that the set of all integers, denoted by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, is an infinite set. \n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Statement\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Statement</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Assumption\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Assumption</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Identifying_a_Maximum\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Identifying a Maximum</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Construction_of_Counterexample\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Construction of Counterexample</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Contradiction\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Contradiction</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The set of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integers</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/4/ae4f90c44600f225dda78864305e7f233bdddbcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z} = \\{\\dots, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, \\dots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"227\" height=\"20\" /> contains infinitely many elements. Equivalently, there does not exist a finite integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that represents the total cardinality of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, nor does the set possess an absolute maximum or minimum element.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We proceed using a classical <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Proof_by_contradiction\" title=\"Proof by contradiction\">proof by contradiction</a>.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Assumption\">Assumption</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Assumption\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Assume for the sake of contradiction that the set of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is strictly finite. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Identifying_a_Maximum\">Identifying a Maximum</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Identifying a Maximum\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is assumed to be a finite set of real numbers and contains elements greater than zero (such as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), by the well-ordering principle or the properties of finite sets, it must be bounded above. Therefore, there must exist a unique maximum element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/d/31dee659f7834aa7cf545d1e8fa89bab9af82806.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" /> such that:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/e/8eefc6159f5ff5577da12c03334bf8cd3f17269b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\quad x \\le M\\]\" width=\"133\" height=\"16\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Construction_of_Counterexample\">Construction of Counterexample</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Construction of Counterexample\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Now, consider a new element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b4f897205beb16e266ea66d8ae8ece27cf5af8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39;$\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" /> constructed by adding the identity element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to our maximum element:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/b/ebb51b22f19c7b5720d9e9420df82919839d1347.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M&#39; = M + 1\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>We analyze the properties of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b4f897205beb16e266ea66d8ae8ece27cf5af8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39;$\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Closure under Addition:</b> Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/d/31dee659f7834aa7cf545d1e8fa89bab9af82806.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/1/dc1b1cb076349218b6506440707f8481288901b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" />, and the integers are closed under the operation of addition, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/2/e92699dbaeacc13b8805b16c89430f8a6f0d4279.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39; \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"17\" />.</li>\n<li><b>Strict Inequality:</b> By the ordering axioms of the integers, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/9/ef9b46289aad6bf123209c32906a8d1688478d65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />, we can add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> to both sides of the inequality to yield:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/6/e760219f8b86983243e6835a4af9c1eda5cc121c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M + 1 &gt; M \\implies M&#39; &gt; M\\]\" width=\"211\" height=\"15\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Contradiction\">Contradiction</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Contradiction\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We have shown that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b4f897205beb16e266ea66d8ae8ece27cf5af8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39;$\" width=\"23\" height=\"14\" /> is an element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/3/cf3e93187434a8f2e1661062755a28fba10988be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39; &gt; M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"17\" />. This directly contradicts our initial governing condition that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is the maximum element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> (which requires all elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/8/e58b46c4431936312fa198e6b957fb42a05bc733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> to satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/f/40f6a62895bb4a5f7dae48991241a9ca8d87b718.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\le M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"17\" />). \n</p><p>Because our initial assumption that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is finite leads directly to a logical contradiction, the assumption must be false. \n</p><p>Hence, the set of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is infinite. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Proof_by_contradiction\" title=\"Proof by contradiction\">Proof by contradiction</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclid%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Euclid&#39;s Theorem\">Euclid's Theorem</a> (Infinitude of primes)</li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property\" title=\"Archimedean property\">Archimedean property</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set_theory\" title=\"Set theory\">Set theory</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017234607\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.020 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.063 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 174/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 307/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5158/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.334      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.334      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37628:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017234607 and revision id 291211. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}